# minigbm's gralloc mapper is a same-process HAL: it is loaded INTO
# SurfaceFlinger, bootanim, system_server and every app process, and on init it
# enumerates /dev/dri. That is why the same denial appears for a dozen
# unrelated domains -- it is not "graphics services need graphics access", it is
# every GL client doing device enumeration in-process.
allow { appdomain surfaceflinger bootanim system_server } gpu_device:dir r_dir_perms;
allow { appdomain surfaceflinger bootanim system_server } graphics_device:chr_file getattr;

# gralloc needs the CARD node, not just the render node, on any GPU whose
# minigbm backend is the dumb-buffer one -- which is every driver without a
# backend of its own, nouveau included.
#
# cros_gralloc_driver's init_try_nodes() opens render nodes first, and minigbm's
# dumb backend is built entirely on DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB. That ioctl is
# declared with no flags (drm_ioctl.c:708) while drm_ioctl_permit() refuses any
# ioctl not marked DRM_RENDER_ALLOW to a render client (drm_ioctl.c:627-630), so
# on renderD* it can only ever return -EACCES. i915 and amdgpu never notice --
# their backends allocate through render-allowed driver-specific GEM ioctls --
# but on an NVIDIA machine every allocation failed:
#     [minigbm:drv_dumb_bo_create_ex(395)]: CREATE_DUMB failed (4, 13)   <- EACCES
#     GraphicBufferAllocator: Failed to allocate (384 x 384) ... : -12
#     F SurfaceFlinger: output buffer not gpu writeable
#
# dumb_driver.c now probes CREATE_DUMB in its init and declines a node that
# cannot serve it, so init_try_nodes() falls through to the card node. This is
# the other half of that: without it the fallback open is denied and gralloc
# ends up with no device at all, which is worse than the render node.
#
# hal_graphics_allocator already holds gpu_device rw (prebuilts .../202504/
# private/hal_graphics_allocator.te:10-11); card nodes are labelled
# graphics_device by our file_contexts:7, and only untrusted_app, isolated_app
# and shell are neverallow'd it (app.te:606).
allow hal_graphics_allocator graphics_device:chr_file rw_file_perms;

# The GPU's PCI uevent read that libdrm needs is handled by labelling the
# subtree sysfs_pci -- see sepolicy/device.te, genfs_contexts and
# surfaceflinger.te. Apps keep the denial: extending any sysfs read to
# appdomain is refused by five separate neverallows (sdk_sandbox_all,
# isolated_app, ephemeral_app, pcc_component, coredomain), and libdrm falls
# back to its ioctl probe there.

# FMQ over memfd: the message queues audio and power HALs share with their
# clients are memfds, which carry the plain tmpfs label.
allow { audioserver system_server } tmpfs:file { read write map };

# SwiftShader JITs its shaders, so SurfaceFlinger needs execmem whenever ANGLE
# is the GL implementation (i.e. on real hardware, where Mesa has no driver).
# PRODUCT_REQUIRES_INSECURE_EXECMEM_FOR_SWIFTSHADER only reaches system_server.te
# -- policy.go passes it as target_requires_insecure_execmem_for_swiftshader and
# nothing in surfaceflinger.te consults it -- so SF needs this spelled out:
#     avc: denied { execmem } scontext=u:r:surfaceflinger:s0  (956 times)
allow surfaceflinger self:process execmem;


# bootanim needs execmem for the same reason SurfaceFlinger does, and without
# it the boot animation does not merely fail to appear -- it crashes:
#     avc: denied { execmem } scontext=u:r:bootanim:s0 tclass=process
#     F libc: Fatal signal 11 (SIGSEGV), code 2 (SEGV_ACCERR) in
#             tid 1126 (Thread<00>), pid 1117 (bootanimation)
#
# bootanim is a GL client like any other: ANGLE loads SwiftShader in-process,
# SwiftShader JITs its shaders, and the JIT needs writable-executable pages.
# The grant for surfaceflinger above already explains why
# PRODUCT_REQUIRES_INSECURE_EXECMEM_FOR_SWIFTSHADER does not cover this --
# policy.go only passes it through to system_server.te -- so every other
# SwiftShader client has to be named explicitly. bootanim was simply missed.
#
# Note this is not the whole story for the boot animation. The log also says
# "BootAnimation: No animation file": no bootanimation.zip is shipped, so even
# without the crash there would be nothing to draw. That is a cosmetic asset
# and a separate matter from the segfault.
allow bootanim self:process execmem;
